@prefix agp: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov#> .
@prefix bl: <https://dagalog.no/ns/backlog#> .
@prefix ghissues: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/issues/> .
@prefix ghpull: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/pull/> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix session: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov/session#> .

# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch issue-569-gene-ontology-test); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:602 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "scripts: fix stale/wrong-format go.ttl silently accepted by download script" ;
    bl:number 602 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:569 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr602 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:569 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-26T20:17:11Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr602Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #569 reported the ignored Gene Ontology performance tests failing with a TurtleSyntaxError at byte offset 0..21, 'Invalid IRI code point ' ''. The obvious hypothesis (a corrupted download) had to be verified, not assumed, per the task brief. Reproduced the exact byte-for-byte error by copying the downloaded go.owl.xml to go.ttl and running the test — confirmed offset 0..21 is precisely the length of '<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>'. To rule out a real turtle-parser bug, downloaded a portable JDK 21 + Apache Jena 6.2.0 (no sudo available in this environment) and ran the actual riot --output=TURTLE conversion on the real go.owl.xml; the resulting genuine go.ttl parsed cleanly through dagalog's oxttl-based parser (1,445,043 triples, zero errors). This located the real defect: scripts/download_test_ontologies.sh's riot-not-found fallback message instructed users to save go.owl (OWL/XML despite the extension) directly as go.ttl — exactly the corruption reproduced above. Fixed by removing that wrong instruction (confirming via curl that no direct Turtle release exists at either candidate URL, so riot really is required), adding a dependency-free is_turtle_file() sanity check (scripts/lib/is_turtle_file.sh, unit-tested in scripts/test_is_turtle_file.sh) that rejects XML-looking content, and making the riot conversion atomic (write to .tmp, validate, then mv) so a mid-conversion crash can no longer leave a corrupt file that a later run's 'already present' check would silently accept." ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr602Decision ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:602 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr602 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr602Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose to actually run riot (via a portable, no-sudo-required JDK 21 + Jena 6.2.0 download) against the real go.owl.xml and feed the genuine output through dagalog's own oxttl-based parser, rather than declaring the turtle parser innocent by inspection or by trusting that oxttl 'should' support riot's SPARQL-style BASE/PREFIX Turtle output. A standalone oxttl test on synthetic BASE/PREFIX input alone parsed fine, which was tempting to treat as sufficient — but the task explicitly required verifying against reality, and only running the real conversion pipeline end-to-end (1,445,043 triples, zero errors) constituted that proof." ;
    agp:alternative "Trusting the synthetic 'BASE <...>\\nPREFIX p: <...>\\n:x a :Foo .' unit test as proof the parser was innocent, without ever running the real riot binary against the real 129 MB go.owl.xml — rejected because it would not have ruled out riot itself producing different/malformed output on the full file, and the task brief explicitly demanded verification, not a plausible-sounding theory." .
